1.  Introduction
SmartBistro is a full-featured, web-based Restaurant Management System (RMS) designed to consolidate guest ordering, kitchen execution, inventory control, and business analytics into a single, cohesive platform. This report constitutes the complete project documentation for Assessment 3 of SENG205 at Kent Institute Australia, extending the initial design produced in Assessment 2 into a fully realised software engineering artefact.
Modern hospitality venues face mounting operational pressure: rising labour costs, shrinking margins, and customer expectations shaped by digital-first experiences (Sommerville 2016, p. 4). Fragmented tooling — siloed POS systems, paper dockets, manual stock counts — compounds these pressures by introducing latency, data loss, and communication gaps (Kotonya & Sommerville 1998, p. 3). SmartBistro addresses each failure mode through purpose-built modules that share a unified data layer, ensuring that a single guest order simultaneously updates the kitchen queue, decrements ingredient stock, and contributes to management analytics.
This document covers: detailed requirements elicitation and analysis; use case and sequence diagrams; high-level and detailed component design; justification of the Agile Scrum methodology and modern tooling choices; a hi-fidelity prototype overview; code repository and project management strategy; a comprehensive test plan; and an updated budget and project timeline. Together these artefacts demonstrate the full software engineering lifecycle from requirements through to testable, deployable software.
2.  Requirements Elicitation
2.1  Elicitation Methodology
Requirements were gathered through a triangulated elicitation process to maximise coverage and accuracy. First, the formal project brief (Kent Institute Australia 2026) was analysed to extract mandated functional areas. Second, competitive system analysis was conducted across Toast POS, MarketMan, and Deputy to identify established patterns in restaurant workflow software. Third, a structured walkthrough of a typical full-service restaurant day — from opening prep to post-service reconciliation — was used to surface implicit operational needs not stated in the brief.
This approach aligns with Kotonya and Sommerville's (1998, p. 55) recommendation that requirements engineers employ multiple elicitation techniques, as no single method captures the complete stakeholder perspective. Particular attention was paid to edge cases: concurrent order submission under peak load, partial ingredient availability at time of order, and failure modes within the payment gateway. These scenarios directly informed both the functional requirements and the concurrency non-functional requirement (NFR-2).
