2.3  Non-Functional Requirements
Non-functional requirements (NFRs) define the quality attributes that constrain system behaviour. The most critical NFRs for SmartBistro are performance (NFR-1: API response under 500ms at normal load), concurrency control (NFR-2: atomic inventory deductions to prevent race conditions), ACID compliance (NFR-3: PostgreSQL or MySQL for payment and stock transactions), and security (NFR-4: bcrypt-hashed passwords and JWT-protected API routes). These form the quality gates that each sprint release must satisfy before code is merged into the main branch (Pressman & Maxim 2015, p. 147).
 
3.  Requirements Analysis & Prioritisation
3.1  MoSCoW Method — Application and Critique
Requirements were prioritised using the MoSCoW framework (Agile Business Consortium 2023), which classifies each requirement as Must Have, Should Have, Could Have, or Won't Have for this release. The method was selected because it produces clear, stakeholder-intelligible priority buckets that map naturally onto sprint capacity planning: Must Haves fill Sprint 1, Should Haves target Sprints 2–3, and Could Haves form a managed backlog.
Core Must-Have requirements include QR ordering (FR-1.1–1.3), the KDS order queue and allergen alerts (FR-2.1–2.4), recipe-to-stock mapping and atomic inventory deduction (FR-3.1–3.2), and the security and concurrency NFRs (NFR-2, NFR-3, NFR-4). These represent the minimum viable product (MVP) — the system cannot serve a single guest without them.
Strengths of MoSCoW: The method is fast to apply, easy for non-technical stakeholders to understand, and integrates naturally with Scrum's sprint planning ceremonies (Agile Business Consortium 2023). It encourages the team to make explicit trade-off decisions rather than treating all requirements as equal.
Limitations of MoSCoW: Critics note that MoSCoW can produce priority inflation — teams may assign too many Must Haves, eroding the distinction (Wiegers & Beatty 2013, p. 319). The method also lacks a quantitative dimension; two Must-Have requirements with vastly different implementation effort are treated identically. To mitigate this, the team supplemented MoSCoW with an Impact vs. Effort matrix, enabling effort-sensitive sequencing within each bucket.
3.2  Impact vs. Effort Matrix
Plotting requirements on a two-dimensional Impact vs. Effort matrix revealed four quadrants. High-Impact / Low-Effort "quick wins" — QR ordering UI, floor plan display, and KDS order queue — are targeted in Sprint 1. High-Impact / High-Effort "major projects" — payment gateway integration, atomic concurrency control, and the analytics dashboard — are distributed across Sprints 2 and 3. Low-Impact items are deferred to the backlog, preventing scope creep in early sprints (Sommerville 2016, p. 89).
3.3  Alternative Prioritisation Methods Considered
The team considered but rejected the Weighted Shortest Job First (WSJF) method used in SAFe, which ranks features by the ratio of cost of delay to job size. While WSJF provides finer-grained sequencing, it requires quantitative cost-of-delay estimates that are impractical to derive in an academic prototype context. MoSCoW with effort supplementation delivered sufficient precision for a 12-week development horizon.
4.  High-Level System Architecture
4.1  Architectural Style
SmartBistro adopts a layered, RESTful service architecture. The presentation layer (React.js SPA and server-side-rendered mobile menu pages) communicates with a stateless API Gateway, which routes requests to domain-specific backend services implemented in either Spring Boot (Java) or Django REST Framework (Python) as finalised by the team during Sprint 0 toolchain selection. All services share a single PostgreSQL database, with Redis used as an in-process cache for session tokens and as a message broker for real-time KDS push events via WebSocket.
This architecture was chosen because it separates concerns at well-defined boundaries, enabling independent testing of each module (Pressman & Maxim 2015, p. 202). The stateless API tier supports horizontal scaling (NFR-6), satisfying peak-load requirements without architectural rework. WebSocket channels provide the sub-two-second KDS update latency required by milestone M3.
4.2  Detailed Component Design
The system is composed of five primary components:
•	Guest Ordering Service: Handles QR code generation, menu retrieval, order submission, and Stripe payment integration. Exposes endpoints POST /orders, GET /menu/{tableId}, and POST /payments.
•	Table Management Service: Maintains real-time table state via a WebSocket-broadcast event bus. Provides REST endpoints for floor-plan CRUD and reservation management.
•	Kitchen Display Service: Subscribes to new-order events, ranks orders by prep-time estimate using a priority queue, and broadcasts status changes to all connected KDS clients and FoH staff via WebSocket.
•	Inventory & Waste Service: Consumes order-confirmed events and executes atomic SQL UPDATE transactions (wrapped in database-level SERIALIZABLE isolation) to deduct ingredients. Exposes waste-logging endpoints and computes true COGS as (Opening Stock + Purchases) - (Closing Stock + Waste).
•	Analytics & Loyalty Service: Aggregates sales data via scheduled jobs and exposes dashboard endpoints. Manages the customer loyalty ledger, computing point accruals per-order and processing redemptions at checkout.
Each service is independently deployable, covered by its own unit-test suite, and integrated through a shared database schema governed by foreign-key relationships defined in the ERD (Assessment 2, Figure 1).
 
