The diagram highlights three key actor-use-case relationships that drive the must-have feature set. The Guest actor initiates the core transaction loop: Scan QR & View Menu → Place Order → Make Payment, with an optional branch to Earn Loyalty Points when a customer profile exists. Kitchen Staff interact exclusively with the KDS subsystem (View KDS Queue, Update Order Status), enforcing a clean separation of concerns between the front-of-house and back-of-house workflows. The Manager actor is the sole initiator of analytics and reporting use cases, reflecting the role-based access control model enforced by JWT authentication (NFR-4).
6.  Sequence Diagram
Figure 2 details the end-to-end message flow for the core Guest Order Placement & KDS Update scenario. This flow exercises every system component and is the primary integration test target for Sprint 1.

 
Figure 2: Sequence Diagram — Guest Order Placement and KDS Update Flow (14 messages across 5 participants).
The diagram reveals two technically significant design decisions. First, the deductStock() self-call at step 7 (Order Service) occurs within a database transaction protected by SERIALIZABLE isolation, directly implementing the atomicity requirement of NFR-2. Second, the KDS notification at step 9 uses an asynchronous push mechanism (Redis pub/sub → WebSocket broadcast) rather than synchronous HTTP, ensuring that the API response at step 11 is not blocked by the KDS update latency. This decoupled design also means that a KDS client failure does not cause order creation to fail — a resilience requirement identified during the edge-case walkthrough in Section 2.1.
 
7.  Development Methodology & Modern Tools
7.1  Agile Scrum — Extended Justification
As established in Assessment 2, the team has adopted Agile Scrum with three two-week sprints following a Sprint 0 setup phase. Schwaber and Sutherland (2020, p. 3) define Scrum as a lightweight framework for generating value through adaptive solutions, making it well-suited to a project where real-time technical constraints (WebSocket latency, atomic transactions) can only be validated through working code rather than upfront specification.
The choice of Scrum over a sequential Waterfall model reflects Sommerville's (2016, p. 68) observation that incremental delivery is particularly valuable when requirements involve user interface design — a domain where stakeholder feedback on working prototypes consistently outperforms feedback on written specifications. SmartBistro's KDS touchscreen workflow (NFR-5) and mobile ordering flow are prime examples: usability issues would likely only be surfaced through a working prototype, not through requirement documents.
Sprint cadence maps directly to assessment milestones, as detailed in Section 9. The rotating Scrum Master role distributes project management overhead equitably across team members, aligning with the group contribution requirements of this assessment.
