8.  Hi-Fidelity Prototype Overview
A hi-fidelity interactive prototype was developed in Figma prior to frontend implementation, applying the design principles of visual hierarchy, affordance, feedback, and consistency (Norman 2013, p. 72). The prototype covers four primary screens:
•	Guest Mobile Menu: Full-bleed hero imagery per dish category, bottom-sheet ingredient modifier panel for dietary customisations, persistent order summary drawer accessible throughout browsing. Typography follows a two-level hierarchy — 20pt bold dish names over 14pt regular descriptors — to guide attention at arm's-length mobile viewing distance.
•	Interactive Floor Plan: Bird's-eye grid of tables colour-coded by status (green = available, amber = occupied, red = dirty, blue = reserved). Tap-to-cycle status transitions are animated with 150ms spring easing to provide tactile feedback on the KDS touchscreen (NFR-5).
•	Kitchen Display System: Dark-mode interface to reduce eye strain in low-light kitchen environments. Orders are displayed as card tiles ranked by prep-time countdown timer. Allergen alerts render as a pulsing red badge to attract immediate attention, implementing FR-2.3.
•	Analytics Dashboard: Line charts for daily/weekly revenue trends rendered via a charting library, with a filterable heat-map grid for peak-hour analysis and a sortable top-dishes table. Export buttons trigger PDF and CSV generation from pre-computed aggregates, satisfying FR-4.1 and FR-4.2.
Design decisions were validated through peer walkthroughs within the team, and the resulting wireframes were used as the direct specification for React component development, minimising the gap between design intent and implementation.
 
9.  Code Repository & Project Management
9.1  Git Branching Strategy
The team follows a feature-branch workflow. The main branch is protected — direct pushes are disabled and at least one peer review approval is required before a pull request can be merged. Each sprint produces a release branch (release/sprint-1, release/sprint-2, etc.) tagged with a semantic version on code freeze. Feature branches follow the naming convention feature/{ticket-id}-{short-description}, enabling automatic ticket linking within GitHub Projects.
Commit messages adhere to the Conventional Commits specification (feat:, fix:, test:, docs:), enabling automatic changelog generation and providing a clear audit trail of contribution history. This directly supports the individual contribution assessment, as each team member's commit history is verifiable against assigned features.
9.2  CI/CD Pipeline
A GitHub Actions workflow triggers on every pull request to main. The pipeline executes: (1) linting (ESLint / pylint), (2) unit tests with coverage reporting, (3) integration tests against a containerised PostgreSQL instance via Docker Compose, and (4) Docker image build. The pipeline fails and blocks merge if test coverage falls below 70% (Milestone M6 criterion) or if any test fails. This continuous integration discipline prevents accumulation of technical debt and ensures that the codebase is in a demonstrable state for each sprint review.
9.3  Sprint Board
GitHub Projects provides a Kanban board with columns: Backlog → Sprint Backlog → In Progress → In Review → Done. Each issue represents a user story with acceptance criteria derived from the functional requirements. Story points are assigned using the Fibonacci sequence during sprint planning, and velocity is tracked sprint-over-sprint to improve estimation accuracy. The Sprint 1 backlog targets 40 story points across three team members, consistent with industry benchmarks of 8–13 points per developer per two-week sprint (Schwaber & Sutherland 2020, p. 10).
10.  Test Plan
10.1  Testing Strategy
SmartBistro's testing strategy adopts a three-layer pyramid: unit tests at the base (targeting individual service methods), integration tests in the middle (covering service-to-database interactions and inter-service calls), and end-to-end tests at the apex (simulating full guest and kitchen user journeys). This hierarchy is consistent with the test automation best practices described by Pressman and Maxim (2015, p. 453), and targets an aggregate coverage of at least 70% by the final release.
Non-functional testing is conducted as a separate stream: k6 load tests verify the 500ms API response NFR under simulated concurrent load of 50 users, and a manual security review checks JWT token expiry behaviour and bcrypt cost factor configuration.
