12.  Conclusion
This report has presented a complete project documentation package for SmartBistro, extending the initial requirements and methodology established in Assessment 2 into a fully realised software engineering artefact. The requirements elicitation process, grounded in a triangulated methodology drawing on the project brief, competitor analysis, and operational workflow modelling, produced a prioritised, MoSCoW-classified requirement set with documented trade-offs between alternative prioritisation approaches.
The system architecture — a layered RESTful service model with WebSocket real-time channels — directly addresses the technical risks identified early: concurrent inventory deductions protected by SERIALIZABLE database isolation, sub-two-second KDS latency through asynchronous push, and horizontal scalability via a stateless API tier. Use case and sequence diagrams formalise the system's behavioural specification, providing an unambiguous reference for both development and acceptance testing.
The 15-case test plan, spanning unit, integration, end-to-end, and load-testing layers, provides measurable quality gates aligned to each functional requirement and NFR. Combined with the Git feature-branch workflow, GitHub Actions CI pipeline, and sprint board discipline, the team is positioned to deliver a production-grade prototype by Week 12 that meets the assessment criteria and demonstrates the full software engineering lifecycle from conception through to tested, deployable software.
 
