Individual Reflection — Bidhan Gurung
Assessment 3 built directly on the foundations laid in Assessment 2, but demanded a qualitatively different mode of thinking. Where Assessment 2 asked "what should the system do?", Assessment 3 required answering "how should it do it — and how do we know it works?" This shift from requirements to design and verification exposed the most intellectually challenging aspects of software engineering for me personally.
Producing the sequence diagram was where this challenge was felt most acutely. Translating the order-placement use case into a precise sequence of numbered messages across five participants forced me to think concurrently about API contract design, database transaction boundaries, and WebSocket event timing simultaneously. Pressman and Maxim (2015, p. 202) describe this kind of interaction modelling as the bridge between architectural intent and implementable code, and I now understand why: every ambiguity in a sequence diagram becomes a bug in production.
Designing the test plan was equally illuminating. Writing TC-03 — the concurrent stock deduction test — required me to fully understand the database-level mechanism (SERIALIZABLE isolation) that prevents the race condition specified in NFR-2. It is one thing to state "inventory deductions must be atomic" in a requirements document; it is another to design a repeatable test that can prove or disprove that property. This experience reinforced for me that testability is not a property you add at the end of development — it must be designed in from the beginning, a principle Sommerville (2016, p. 218) terms "design for testability".
From a team perspective, the rotating Scrum Master structure worked well for distributing ownership of the report. Each member taking the role for one sprint produced a genuine sense of shared responsibility that I did not expect from an academic group project. The decision to use GitHub Projects as our sprint board, rather than a separate tool, also eliminated the synchronisation overhead that often plagues distributed teams — every issue referenced the exact commit that resolved it, providing an unambiguous audit trail of individual contributions.
Overall, Assessment 3 has given me a working understanding of the full pre-implementation documentation lifecycle: from elicitation through prioritisation, architectural design, interface modelling, and test specification. These are skills I expect to use immediately in professional practice.
